#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Not systemd. This exists because `systemctl start something` is the first
# thing anyone tries in a shell that looks this much like a normal Fedora, and
# "command not found" explains nothing about what to do instead.
#
# If a real systemctl ever turns up - someone installs the systemd package -
# defer to it rather than standing in its way. It still will not work (systemd
# is not PID 1 here and cannot be), but that is then its own error to give.
set -uo pipefail

for real in /usr/bin/systemctl /bin/systemctl; do
    if [ -x "${real}" ] && [ "${real}" != "$0" ]; then
        exec "${real}" "$@"
    fi
done

cat >&2 <<'MESSAGE'
There is no systemd in this add-on, so systemctl cannot work: this is a
Supervisor-managed container whose lifecycle Home Assistant owns, and PID 1 is
a minimal init, not systemd. Unit files that packages install are inert.

Background daemons are managed with addon-service instead. A service is an
executable file in /config/services that runs in the foreground; the add-on
starts it at boot and restarts it if it exits:

  addon-service examples          ready-made ones, tailscaled included
  addon-service install <name>    copy one into /config/services
  addon-service list              what exists and what is running
  addon-service start|stop <name>
  addon-service logs <name> -f

For a daemon with no ready-made service, the ExecStart line of the unit file
the package shipped is usually the whole content of the script - as long as it
stays in the foreground. See the add-on documentation, "Running your own
daemons".
MESSAGE
exit 1
